WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.414
Transfer is the ability to use learning outside the original lesson.

2
00:00:04.414 --> 00:00:09.229
Near transfer happens when the new situation closely resembles the practice activity.

3
00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:14.446
Far transfer requires the learner to adapt the principle to a different context.

4
00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:19.662
Both matter, but real work often demands more than repeating the same example.

5
00:00:19.662 --> 00:00:26.082
Conditions are messy, information is incomplete, priorities compete, and the learner has to decide what applies.

6
00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:31.700
Courses support transfer when they include realistic scenarios, meaningful decision points, and context variation.

7
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:38.522
Instead of showing only one perfect example, the learning experience can show several versions of the problem.

8
00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:41.331
What changes when the data is incomplete?

9
00:00:41.331 --> 00:00:43.738
What changes when time is limited?

10
00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:47.350
What signal tells the learner which method to use?

11
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:54.171
Variation helps learners recognize the underlying pattern rather than memorizing the surface details of a single exercise.

12
00:00:54.171 --> 00:00:58.585
Job aids, checklists, examples, and workflow references can also support transfer.

13
00:00:58.585 --> 00:01:01.795
They are not a sign that learning failed.

14
00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:10.623
They are part of good performance design, especially for tasks that are complex, infrequent, safety-sensitive, or easy to confuse under pressure.

15
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:21.859
The point is to help people use what they learned when the course is no longer open and the work has to be done in the real environment.

16
00:01:21.859 --> 00:01:28.680
A strong transfer question is, where will this learner first need the skill after the lesson ends?

17
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.687
That question changes design decisions.

18
00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:34.700
It affects examples, practice conditions, language, timing, and support materials.

19
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:49.145
If the first real use happens during a busy shift, a complex review, or a handoff between teams, the course should prepare learners for that context instead of only preparing them for a clean classroom version.
